---
/**
 * website/src/pages/security.astro — the security page (website plan, Task W1.8; the
 * owner's brief and ruling D189 of 2026-10-19).
 *
 * **The page is three things, and nothing else**, and all three of them come from the
 * document:
 *
 *   1. the **summary** is `SECURITY.md`'s own "In short" section, rendered from the
 *      generated copy of the document;
 *   2. the **"Reporting a vulnerability" section**, rendered from the same copy;
 *   3. a **link to `SECURITY.md`** in the repository.
 *
 * The page used to render `SECURITY.md` in full, behind a contents list built from its
 * headings. The owner cut it to those three things, and D189 settled the one open question —
 * where the summary comes from: **each long page's summary is rendered from a file, never
 * written on the page**, and for this page the file is `SECURITY.md` itself, whose short
 * "In short" section is the summary the page renders, written or approved by the owner as
 * every `SECURITY.md` change is (`docs/website/redesign/reconciliation.md`, D189). So the
 * page carries no summary prose of its own: a sentence here that the document does not state
 * is the one thing a security page may not have (D99), and there is now nowhere to write one.
 *
 * Four decisions are visible in the file:
 *
 *   1. **Both sections are the document's, word for word under the renderer.** They are
 *      extracted from the generated `website/content/security.md` — the file `go run
 *      ./internal/clidoc` writes from `SECURITY.md`, which `loadSite()` validates and a
 *      missing or hand-edited copy of stops the build (W1.3 step 2) — so what the page says
 *      cannot drift from the file it links to. A document that renamed, moved or emptied
 *      either section stops the build rather than shipping a page with a hole in it.
 *      `website/test/security.mjs` holds both to the document, bullet for bullet and
 *      paragraph for paragraph.
 *   2. **A cross-reference inside the document becomes a link into the document.** The "In
 *      short" bullets each end by naming the section they come from
 *      (`([Known limits](#known-limits))`), and the page renders two of those sections and no
 *      others. A reference to one of the two the page carries — today the summary's
 *      `[Reporting a vulnerability](#reporting-a-vulnerability)` — is a link to the page's own
 *      anchor, because that is where the section is and where a reader can follow it; a
 *      reference to any other section is rewritten to the document in the repository at the
 *      section it names. A cross-reference the document has **no heading for** is rendered as
 *      its own text and
 *      not as a link at all (round 4 of the review): every target the page can build for it
 *      is wrong — the fragment as written is a link that goes nowhere, `website/test/
 *      linkcheck.mjs` refuses a GitHub link whose fragment is not a heading, and the
 *      document's own URL sends the reader to the top of the file with a link that reads
 *      like every other one and arrives nowhere near the section it names. Text promises
 *      nothing. `SECURITY.md` ships one (`[Money](#money)`, whose target is a bold lead in a
 *      bullet under "Known limits" and not a heading); the owner has it in the pull request,
 *      because the fix is a `SECURITY.md` edit. `website/test/security.mjs` pins the whole
 *      rule over the built page — every cross-reference's text and order against the
 *      document's own links, its href against the heading its fragment names (the page's own
 *      anchor where the page renders that heading, the document's URL otherwise), and the
 *      fragment-less one against the fact that it is not a link — so a fragment dropped,
 *      aimed at another heading, sent to GitHub though the page carries it, or rendered as an
 *      anchor fails there.
 *   3. **The link is the repository's, and its path is read rather than typed.** The URL is
 *      built from `site.yaml`'s repository and the generated file's own `source:` header, so
 *      the page cannot point at a document this repository does not carry.
 *   4. **The styles are global and token-only.** The rendered sections arrive through
 *      `set:html`, which Astro's scoped styles do not reach, so the block below is
 *      `is:global` and every selector is prefixed with the page's own marker attribute so it
 *      cannot reach another page. Colours come from W1.2's semantic tokens and never from a
 *      literal here.
 *
 * Nothing in this file fetches anything, reads a clock or starts a process: the page is
 * built from the files in this repository (Global Constraints 4 and 5).
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { loadSite } from '../content.ts'
import { headingAnchor, headingText } from '../lib/github-slug.mjs'

// The whole generated layer, validated: a missing or hand-edited security.md, or any other
// fact the site renders, fails here with the file's name in the message (see 1 above).
const site = loadSite()
const securityDocument = site.documents['security.md']

// The link's target: the file this page renders, in the repository `site.yaml` names. The
// path is the generated file's own `source:` fact, so neither the repository nor the path is
// written here, and `main` is the branch those files live on — the repository's default
// branch, which every other GitHub link the site builds also names.
const sourceHref = new URL(`blob/main/${securityDocument.source}`, `${site.facts.repository}/`).href

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The document's own sections, and the markup they are rendered with.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** The two sections the page renders, as the document spells their headings. */
const SUMMARY = 'In short'
const REPORTING = 'Reporting a vulnerability'
const SUMMARY_ID = 'in-short'
const REPORTING_ID = 'reporting-a-vulnerability'

/**
 * The document's lines under its `## <heading>`, up to the next heading of the same level or
 * higher. A document without the heading fails the build: the page renders both sections and
 * has nothing to fall back on, so a document that moved one stops the build rather than
 * shipping a page with a hole in it.
 */
function sectionLines(heading: string): string[] {
  const lines = securityDocument.body.split('\n')
  const start = lines.findIndex((line) => line.trim() === `## ${heading}`)
  if (start === -1) {
    throw new Error(
      `${securityDocument.path} has no "## ${heading}" heading, and this page renders that section of it. The document is the page's whole text (D189), so a document that moved the section stops the build.`,
    )
  }
  const rest = lines.slice(start + 1)
  const end = rest.findIndex((line) => /^#{1,2}[ \t]/.test(line))
  return end === -1 ? rest : rest.slice(0, end)
}

/** The anchors the document's own headings carry, the way GitHub slugs them. */
const documentAnchors = new Set(
  securityDocument.body
    .split('\n')
    .map((line) => /^#{1,6}[ \t]+(.*)$/.exec(line.trim()))
    .filter((match): match is RegExpExecArray => match !== null)
    .map((match) => headingAnchor(headingText(match[1]))),
)

/**
 * The anchors **this page itself renders**: the two sections it carries, under the ids the
 * markup above gives their headings. A cross-reference to one of them is a link a reader can
 * follow here, and it must be the page's own anchor rather than the document's — the section
 * is on this page, and sending the reader to GitHub for a section they are looking at is the
 * wrong destination even though the fragment would resolve there too.
 */
const pageAnchors = new Set([SUMMARY_ID, REPORTING_ID])

/**
 * A link target in the document, as the page renders it. A path is the document's own and
 * is left as it is; a cross-reference into a section **this page renders** becomes a link to
 * the page's own anchor, which is the section a reader can reach from here (the summary's
 * `[Reporting a vulnerability](#reporting-a-vulnerability)` is that case, and it is the page's
 * own `<h2 id="reporting-a-vulnerability">` that answers it); a cross-reference into any other
 * section of the document becomes a link to the document in the repository, at the section it
 * names. A fragment the document has **no heading for** has no target at all (`null`), and
 * `inline()` renders that reference as its own text: the only link the page could build for it
 * would send a reader to the top of the file, or to a fragment nothing answers, which is a
 * promise the page cannot keep.
 */
function target(raw: string): string | null {
  if (!raw.startsWith('#')) return raw
  const fragment = raw.slice(1)
  if (pageAnchors.has(fragment)) return `#${fragment}`
  return documentAnchors.has(fragment) ? `${sourceHref}#${fragment}` : null
}

/** The document's inline markup, as HTML. */
function escapeHTML(text: string): string {
  return text
    .replace(/&/g, '&amp;')
    .replace(/</g, '&lt;')
    .replace(/>/g, '&gt;')
    .replace(/"/g, '&quot;')
}

/**
 * One line's inline markup: code spans, bold, italics and links, in the order the document
 * nests them, with everything else escaped — and nothing added, so a space the document
 * writes is a space the page renders. The subset is what the document's two sections use and
 * nothing else is guessed at, because a page that mangles a security sentence is worse than
 * one that does not render it (D99).
 */
function inline(text: string): string {
  let html = ''
  let plain = ''
  const flush = (): void => {
    html += escapeHTML(plain)
    plain = ''
  }

  let i = 0
  while (i < text.length) {
    const rest = text.slice(i)
    let match: RegExpExecArray | null = null

    if (text[i] === '`' && (match = /^`([^`]+)`/.exec(rest)) !== null) {
      flush()
      html += `<code>${escapeHTML(match[1])}</code>`
      i += match[0].length
      continue
    }
    if (rest.startsWith('**') && (match = /^\*\*(.+?)\*\*/.exec(rest)) !== null) {
      flush()
      html += `<strong>${inline(match[1])}</strong>`
      i += match[0].length
      continue
    }
    if (text[i] === '*' && (match = /^\*([^*]+)\*/.exec(rest)) !== null) {
      flush()
      html += `<em>${inline(match[1])}</em>`
      i += match[0].length
      continue
    }
    if (text[i] === '[' && (match = /^\[([^\]]*)\]\(([^)\s]+)\)/.exec(rest)) !== null) {
      flush()
      // The document's own link: its text is rendered as it stands and its target is read
      // through `target()` above. A reference the document has no heading for renders as
      // that text alone — the word, without a promise of where it goes (decision 2).
      const href = target(match[2])
      html += href === null ? inline(match[1]) : `<a href="${escapeHTML(href)}">${inline(match[1])}</a>`
      i += match[0].length
      continue
    }

    plain += text[i]
    i++
  }

  flush()
  return html
}

/**
 * A section's bullets, in the document's order, each one line joined back together when the
 * file wrapped it. A wrap is a line that directly follows a bullet or another wrapped line; a
 * line that follows a **blank** line is a paragraph of its own, and it stops the build, as does
 * a line before the first bullet: the summary is the document's "In short" list, and a
 * paragraph added under it is text the page would otherwise render inside the last bullet's
 * `<li>`, which is text of the owner's the page has no business reshaping (D189).
 */
function bulletLines(heading: string): string[] {
  const bullets: string[] = []
  // Whether the line before this one was a bullet or a wrap of one. A blank line breaks the
  // run, so the line after it is a block of its own rather than a continuation.
  let wrapping = false
  for (const line of sectionLines(heading)) {
    const bullet = /^\s*[-*][ \t]+(.*)$/.exec(line)
    if (bullet !== null) {
      bullets.push(bullet[1].trim())
      wrapping = true
    } else if (line.trim() === '') {
      wrapping = false
    } else if (wrapping) {
      bullets[bullets.length - 1] += ` ${line.trim()}`
    } else {
      throw new Error(
        `${securityDocument.path}'s "## ${heading}" section carries a line that is not a bullet and does not wrap one (${JSON.stringify(line.trim())}), and this page renders the section as the list it is. The document is the page's whole text (D189), so a paragraph beside the list stops the build rather than being rendered as part of the bullet above it.`,
      )
    }
  }
  if (bullets.length === 0) {
    throw new Error(
      `${securityDocument.path}'s "## ${heading}" section carries no bullet, and this page renders it as the list it is: an empty summary is a page that describes nothing (D189).`,
    )
  }
  return bullets
}

/** The summary: the document's own "In short" bullets, each rendered as HTML. */
const summaryBullets = bulletLines(SUMMARY).map(inline)

/**
 * The reporting section's paragraphs, each rendered: the section is two of them today. An
 * emptied section stops the build for the same reason the summary's does — the page would
 * otherwise render a heading over nothing, and a reporter following it would find no
 * instructions where the document promised them (D189).
 */
const reportingParagraphs = sectionLines(REPORTING)
  .join('\n')
  .split(/\n[ \t]*\n/)
  .map((paragraph) => paragraph.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim())
  .filter((paragraph) => paragraph !== '')
  .map(inline)

if (reportingParagraphs.length === 0) {
  throw new Error(
    `${securityDocument.path}'s "## ${REPORTING}" section carries no paragraph, and this page renders that section as the document writes it: an emptied section is a heading over nothing, and the page has no fallback text of its own (D189).`,
  )
}
---

<Base
  title="Security"
  description="Fugaro's security model in short, the document's own instructions for reporting a vulnerability, and a link to the full policy."
>
  <article class="security" data-security-page>
    <h1>Security</h1>

    {/* The document's own summary (D189): the "In short" section of SECURITY.md, rendered
        bullet for bullet. Nothing here is written on this page; the text is the owner's. */}
    <section data-security-summary>
      <h2 id={SUMMARY_ID}>{SUMMARY}</h2>
      <ul>
        {summaryBullets.map((bullet) => <li set:html={bullet} />)}
      </ul>
    </section>

    {/* The document's own reporting section, rendered and not edited. */}
    <section data-security-reporting>
      <h2 id={REPORTING_ID}>{REPORTING}</h2>
      {reportingParagraphs.map((paragraph) => <p set:html={paragraph} />)}
    </section>

    {/* The pointer to the document itself. One sentence, and the page's own. */}
    <p class="policy" data-security-policy>The full policy is <a href={sourceHref} rel="noopener">{securityDocument.source}</a> in the repository.</p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style is:global>
  /* Global, because the rendered sections arrive through `set:html` and Astro's scoped
     styles do not reach markup this page did not write; every selector is prefixed with
     `data-security-page`, so nothing here can reach another page. Colours are W1.2's tokens
     and this file carries no colour literal. */
  [data-security-page] {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: var(--page-max);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-lg) var(--space-sm) var(--space-2xl);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  [data-security-page] h1 {
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-lg);
  }

  [data-security-page] h2 {
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-sm);
  }

  [data-security-summary] {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-lg);
  }

  [data-security-summary] ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  [data-security-summary] li {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-sm);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
  }

  [data-security-summary] strong {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  [data-security-reporting] {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-lg);
  }

  [data-security-reporting] p {
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
  }

  [data-security-page] code {
    background: var(--surface);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: 0.1rem 0.3rem;
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }

  [data-security-page] a {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  [data-security-policy] {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
